<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krasnodar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yekaterinodar, Ekaterinodar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krasnodar is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on the Kuban River and the capital of Krasnodar Krai. It was founded in 1793 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> as a fort named Yekaterinodar, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>, and became the capital of the Kuban Cossack Host. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuban-rada">Kuban Rada</a> and changed hands repeatedly, and it was renamed Krasnodar in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krasnodar.jpg" alt="Krasnodar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maska999 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krasnodar_teatr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Krasnodar stands on the right bank of the Kuban River, about 1,300 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> 120 km to the west. The climate is humid subtropical. Winters are cold and damp with unstable snow cover, the July average is 24.1 °C, and about 735 millimetres of rain and snow fall in a year. No mountains shield the city from cold waves, so winter temperatures below −20 °C are not uncommon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city began in 1793 as a military camp and then a fortress built by the Black Sea Cossacks to defend the imperial borders and to assert Russian dominion over Circassia, a claim that Ottoman Turkey contested. Its name, Yekaterinodar, meant Catherine's Gift, recognising both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>'s grant of land in the Kuban to the Cossacks and Saint Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, regarded as the city's patron. It received town status in 1867 and by 1888 had about 45,000 people. The 1897 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 65,606, of whom 52.9 per cent spoke Russian and 38.3 per cent Ukrainian.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> the city changed hands several times between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteer-army">Volunteer Army</a>. The White general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavr-kornilov">Lavr Kornilov</a> besieged it on 10 April 1918 and was killed a week later when a Bolshevik shell destroyed the farmhouse that served as his headquarters. On 7 December 1920 it was renamed Krasnodar, meaning Gift of the Reds. The city lost over 14 per cent of its population in the Soviet famine of 1932–1933.</p><p>German forces occupied Krasnodar from 9 August 1942 to 12 February 1943. The city was heavily damaged, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> and mobile SS execution squads killed thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Communists and suspected partisans. From 14 to 17 July 1943 a Soviet tribunal at Krasnodar tried people for collaboration and war crimes and pronounced eight death sentences, carried out in the city square before a crowd of about thirty thousand.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Krasnodar is the largest city and administrative centre of Krasnodar Krai, with a population of 1,154,885 within the city limits. The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted one million people there for the first time. The historic centre keeps buildings from the 19th century, and the city has St Catherine's Cathedral and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> lattice hyperboloid tower built by the engineer Vladimir Shukhov in 1928. It is the home of the Kuban Cossack Choir and of the Kovalenko Krasnodar Regional Art Museum, the oldest public art museum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Krasnodar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1792 Catherine II granted the Host the lands of the Taman peninsula and the right bank of the Kuban, and in 1793 they founded <b>Yekaterinodar</b> as their capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/black-sea-cossack-host">Black Sea Cossack Host</a></li><li><blockquote>On 28 May 1918 the Bolsheviks united the Kuban Soviet Republic, centred on <b>Yekaterinodar</b>, and the Black Sea Soviet Republic, based in Novorossiysk, into a single Kuban-Black Sea Soviet Republic, as an autonomous part of the Russian SFSR.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuban-black-sea-soviet-republic">Kuban-Black Sea Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>It was centred on <b>Yekaterinodar</b>, modern Krasnodar.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuban-cossack-host">Kuban Cossack Host</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Russian Revolution the Kuban Cossack Host, descended partly from the Black Sea Cossack Host, set up its own Rada in <b>Yekaterinodar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuban-peoples-republic">Kuban People's Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 1918 Red forces took <b>Yekaterinodar</b> from the Kuban Rada, the Cossack regional government, which fled.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuban-soviet-republic">Kuban Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic was governed by a central executive committee and fought the Volunteer Army of Denikin, which took <b>Yekaterinodar</b> in August 1918 and forced the government to move to Pyatigorsk.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-caucasian-soviet-republic">North Caucasian Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
